{
  "image_name": "20c5c0c1480b46d7a8b9a70556cb7992.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how a Gondwana margin evolved from the Permian–Triassic into the Cretaceous–Paleogene, comparing two branches: the Marie Byrd Land–West Antarctica Rift System (MBL and WARS) and the Antarctic Peninsula. It starts with a subduction margin showing oceanic plate descent, arc magmatism, deformation, and sedimentation along the continental edge.\n\nIn the Jurassic to Early Cretaceous, the left branch shows extension developing behind the margin in MBL and WARS, with labels suggesting inherited older crustal blocks, rifted margin structure, and early partial melting beneath the continental interior. The right branch, in contrast, remains a more typical active continental margin with fore-arc sediments, a Jurassic silicic large igneous province, and back-arc extension causing crustal thinning.\n\nBy the Late Cretaceous to Early Paleogene, the two regions diverge more strongly. In MBL and WARS, the margin appears to have transitioned toward rifting and passive-margin development, with a spreading center, exhumed lower crust, and upper-crustal granites across provinces such as Amundsen and Ross. The Antarctic Peninsula instead continues to show active subduction-related arc magmatism and widespread intrusions or volcanic bodies. Overall, the figure emphasizes contrasting tectonic outcomes from a common earlier convergent setting.",
